        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(tts-contract): cancel unread response body in OpenAI speech provider",
        "body": "## Problem\n\nThe OpenAI TTS contract provider in `src/plugins/contracts/tts-contract-suites.ts` calls `fetch()` against `/audio/speech` to validate the request shape but returns a synthetic `audioBuffer` without consuming or cancelling the response body. On keep-alive HTTP connections the unread body holds the socket open, leaking connection slots in contract test runs that exercise `synthesizeSpeech` and `textToSpeechTelephony` repeatedly.\n\n## Fix\n\nCapture the `Response`, assert `response.ok`, and `await response.body?.cancel()` before returning so the underlying connection is released. The provider still returns the synthetic buffer — only the cleanup is added.\n\n## Real HTTP keep-alive proof\n\nA new test `releases the socket after synthesizeSpeech with a streaming response` starts a real local HTTP server that streams a large response body slowly (64KB chunks every 50ms with keep-alive timeout=30s). The provider calls `fetch()` but returns a synthetic buffer without consuming the stream. After the provider returns:\n\n- **With fix:** `body.cancel()` destroys the socket within ~200ms → `activeConnections` drops to 0\n- **Without fix:** The unread streaming body keeps the socket open → `activeConnections` stays at 1 (leaked)\n\n```\n ✓ |contracts-plugin| ../../src/plugins/contracts/tts.contract.test.ts (44 tests) 5491ms\n     ✓ releases the socket after synthesizeSpeech with a streaming response  639ms\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  44 passed (44)\n```\n\nAdditionally, the `openai provider response body release` mock suite asserts `body.cancel` is called after both `synthesizeSpeech` and `textToSpeechTelephony`.\n\n## Risk\n\nMinimal. The fix only adds `response.body?.cancel()` after the existing fetch and an `ok` check that throws on HTTP errors. The synthetic audio buffer returned to callers is unchanged.",
